{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>A man of the soil.</b> Rashi explained (Rashi on Genesis 9:20): master of the soil, like \"a man of Naomi\" (Ruth 1:3). But this is not correct, for \"a man of Naomi\" is an expression of husbandhood, like \"a man and his wife\" (Genesis 7:2). And others said (the Radak in Sefer haShorashim): the great one of the soil and its chief, and they brought parallels to it: \"Gideon son of Joash, a man of Israel\" (Judges 7:14), \"Both children of Adam and children of man\" (Psalms 49:3), \"Are you not a man, and who is like you in Israel?\" (1 Samuel 26:15), and many others according to their opinion. But in my view, \"Gideon son of Joash, a man of Israel\" is a designation: an Israelite man; \"Are you not a man\" — there is none like you in Israel. And similarly, \"Be strong and be men\" (1 Samuel 4:9) — that they should not be like women. \"Both children of Adam and children of man\" — a certain person known for his excellence. But \"a man of the soil\" is like \"the men of the city\" (Genesis 19:4) — because he dwelt throughout the whole soil; he did not build a city or state to which he might be attributed. And similarly, \"a man of the field\" (Genesis 25:27) — one who stands there all day constantly. And in the Mishnah (Avot 1:4): \"Yose ben Yoezer, a man of Zeredah, and Yose ben Yohanan, a man of Jerusalem.\" Or he devoted his heart to working the soil, to sow and to plant, because he found the earth desolate — for everyone devoted to a particular thing is called thus: \"the men of the city\" — its inhabitants; \"the men of David\" (1 Samuel 23:3) — his servants; \"a man of Elohim\" (Deuteronomy 33:1) — one singled out for His service. And so they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 36:3): \"A man of the soil\" — a burgess by way of being a burgess, and they said there (ibid.) that he was passionately devoted to the soil. And behold, this is a designation. And the sense of \"he began\" (ויחל) is that he began to plant vineyards, for the earlier generations planted a vine, but he began to plant many vines, row upon row, which is called a \"kerem\" (vineyard), for since he desired wine, he did not plant the vine like other trees but made a vineyard."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:21:04.453372958Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}